[Opponent "3.d4 exd4 4.Bc4"]
[Start "scotch-game 3...exd4"]

4. Bc4 { A gambit: White would rather develop than win the pawn back at once. } 4... Bc5 { Develops with a bishop that attacks f2, matching what Black plays against the Italian. } 5. c3 ( 5. Ng5 Nh6 { Guards f7 a second time, so Nxf7 only trades pieces. } 6. O-O ( 6. Qh5 Qf6 { Guards f7 again and eyes f2, so White's attack has run out of pieces. } 7. O-O d6 ) ( 6. Nxf7 Nxf7 7. Bxf7+ Kxf7 8. Qh5+ g6 9. Qxc5 d5 { Opens the c8 bishop and holds the centre. Material is level; Black's king cannot castle, but White has no pieces out to attack it. } ) 6... O-O ) ( 5. O-O d6 { Holds the extra pawn on d4 and opens the c8 bishop. } 6. c3 dxc3 { Takes a second pawn. White gets quick development for it, so Black finishes developing before anything else. } 7. Nxc3 Nf6 ) 5... Nf6 { Hits e4 and makes ...dxc3 unnecessary: Black can give the pawn back or keep it, as it chooses. } 6. cxd4 { This reaches the main line of the Italian d4 line (Bishop on c4, pawns on c3 and d4 traded). One line to learn covers both openings. } ( 6. e5 d5 7. Bb5 Ne4 8. cxd4 Bb6 ) 6... Bb4+ 7. Bd2 Bxd2+ 8. Nbxd2 d5 *
